Discovery   : Training was stable and well generalised. Train smooth_l1 fell 0.236->0.0216 and validation fell 0.0543->0.0220. The final train-val gap was only +0.00033. Validation was best at about epoch 14-15, and the selected checkpoint was epoch 15 (val 0.02189). It then plateaued, drifting only +8e-5 over the last 15 epochs. So there is no overfitting, and the later epochs under the decayed lr added almost nothing. The same capacity would probably work with about 15-20 epochs. The model is small and the gap is tiny, so capacity is not the bottleneck. Gains are more likely to come from the architecture, the input features, or the loss than from longer training. Training ran at the full operator-set formal portion (1.0), so data volume is not a concern.
Memory Update: Record lr_auprc=0.5823 (auroc 0.891, dir_acc 0.806) as the first reference for smooth_l1 with the skip-MLP and zero-init head (8162 params, 30 epochs, lr 0.002 cosine, train loss 0.0216, val ~0.0220). Next steps: (1) Run or retrieve the pure Spearman baseline score at the same formal scope. That is needed to see whether the learned correction adds anything over the baseline. (2) Test a efficiency variant with 15-20 epochs, since val plateaued by epoch ~15. If the score stays within 5%, flag it as a high-efficiency candidate. (3) Change one factor at a time to find real gains, for example latent width or depth, or a different loss_type. Do not compare loss values across different loss types. Formal scope is operator-fixed at 1.0, so do not suggest changing trial_portion for formal rounds. Single-seed score differences under a few percent should be treated cautiously.
